Keep a folder's cached settings until its settings file has been read.

For a remote folder, FolderConfiguration starts on the copy of the folder's
settings that its last load cached, and swaps in a FileServiceBasedConfiguration
once the vscode-remote file system provider registers. It swapped first and let
the new object read the folder's files afterwards, and until that read ends the
new object holds no settings at all: its reparse() answers an empty model.

WorkspaceService.onDefaultConfigurationChanged reparses the folder
configuration and puts the result in place of the folder's and the workspace's
settings. The default configuration changes as the extensions register, since
every extension that contributes settings or configurationDefaults changes it.
So whenever the read was still running when the extensions registered, every
setting of the folder's own was gone from the configuration until the read
ended, and the first reads of whatever started in between, the theme service
and the extensions activating, got the user's values instead.
WorkspaceConfiguration, which does the same for a .code-workspace file, already
loads the new object before swapping it in; this does the same for a folder.

On an Android emulator the read ends seconds after the extensions register. On
API 36 a folder whose .vscode/settings.json names Light Modern, under a user
theme of Dark Modern, showed Dark Modern for 6 to 7.5 s on every load from its
second on: from the extensions registering (code/didLoadExtensions at 12.3 s
in one load) until the read ended (Light Modern back at 20.7 s, at the moment
the explorer filled). The mirror case, a folder naming Dark Modern under Light
Modern, showed Light Modern in the same window. The page script VSCodroid adds
to the workbench page reads which theme a window shows to tell whether its
folder has a theme of its own, and a tap in that window taught it the wrong
answer, which started the next folder on the wrong theme.

The new object is still created, and starts watching the folder's files, as
soon as the provider registers, and a change it reports before the swap is
passed on as before: the workspace service then reloads the cached copy, and
the change that the swap itself reports reloads the files. A folder's first
load, which has no cached copy, is unchanged: it shows the user's settings
until the read ends, as it did.

One note for whoever tidies this later. The swap is written as
`loadConfiguration().then(() => { this.folderConfiguration = ... })` rather
than with await because `.loadConfiguration().then(()=>{this.folderConfiguration=`
is the token patches/fingerprints.txt matches to prove this patch reached the
packaged bundle: esbuild joins the statements after an await with commas, which
the source does not have, so an awaited form leaves no text that is in both the
patch and the bundle. If you rewrite it, move that row to something the new
shape introduces and check the replacement occurs zero times in an unpatched
bundle first.

